WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.519
Social navigation robots have to move safely
through crowds,

00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:09.780
and that means perceiving and predicting the paths
of the people around them.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.377
This paper introduces the SiT dataset,

00:00:12.457 --> 00:00:15.998
short for Socially Interactive Pedestrian Trajectory.

00:00:16.078 --> 00:00:23.513
Unlike earlier trajectory datasets captured from
fixed rooftop cameras or from cars driving on separate roads,

00:00:23.593 --> 00:00:30.892
SiT was recorded by a mobile robot navigating
densely populated indoor and outdoor spaces in downtown Seoul,

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:34.377
capturing genuine human-robot interaction up close.

00:00:34.457 --> 00:00:39.980
It ships synchronized LiDAR, camera, IMU, and RTK
data with 2D and 3D annotations,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.626
semantic maps, and a full benchmark spanning 3D
detection, tracking,

00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:48.520
trajectory prediction, and end-to-end motion forecasting.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.703
For a robot to navigate safely among people,

00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:57.534
it needs to perceive nearby pedestrians as
individual entities in three-dimensional space

00:00:57.614 --> 00:01:00.024
and predict where they will move next.

00:01:00.104 --> 00:01:06.642
The problem is that the datasets used to train
these models don't reflect the robot's real situation.

00:01:06.722 --> 00:01:09.394
Popular pedestrian datasets like ETH, UCY,

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:14.242
and SDD were captured by fixed cameras mounted high
on rooftops or drones,

00:01:14.322 --> 00:01:16.994
so they never see human-robot interaction.

00:01:17.074 --> 00:01:21.056
Large autonomous-driving datasets do provide rich
sensor data,

00:01:21.136 --> 00:01:28.264
but there the vehicle and the pedestrians usually
travel on separate roads and rarely come into close contact.

00:01:28.344 --> 00:01:33.440
Neither setting matches a robot weaving through a
crowded hallway or crosswalk.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.736
Studies of human-robot interaction show that a
robot's motion changes how nearby people walk,

00:01:39.816 --> 00:01:46.312
and these effects are strongest when the robot and
pedestrians share the same space at close distance.

00:01:46.392 --> 00:01:47.859
To study and model that,

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:52.179
we need data collected while a robot actually moves
through crowds,

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:54.694
not from a camera bolted to a building.

00:01:54.774 --> 00:02:01.076
Earlier robot datasets came close but each had a
gap: STCrowd kept its sensors at a fixed position,

00:02:01.156 --> 00:02:02.624
so scenes barely varied,

00:02:02.704 --> 00:02:09.845
and JRDB did not organize its data into
trajectories and did not fully synchronize its multiple sensors in time,

00:02:09.925 --> 00:02:11.585
which limits sensor fusion.

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:14.680
SiT is designed specifically to fill these gaps.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.904
The SiT dataset makes several contributions.

00:02:17.984 --> 00:02:23.751
It provides large-scale real-world pedestrian
trajectories gathered as a robot navigated

00:02:23.831 --> 00:02:27.073
densely populated indoor and outdoor environments,

00:02:27.153 --> 00:02:31.791
from building interiors and campuses to crosswalks
and public walkways.

00:02:31.871 --> 00:02:35.977
It lets researchers build prediction models using
rich context,

00:02:36.057 --> 00:02:41.093
including appearance features, the robot's
ego-motion, and semantic map data.

00:02:41.173 --> 00:02:48.336
It achieves precise time synchronization between
all sensor modalities using a centralized triggering method,

00:02:48.416 --> 00:02:50.728
which makes sensor fusion practical.

00:02:50.808 --> 00:02:55.711
It supplies multi-layered semantic maps for both
indoor and outdoor scenes.

00:02:55.791 --> 00:03:01.159
And it offers a curated benchmark covering 3D
detection, 3D multi-object tracking,

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:06.741
trajectory prediction, and an end-to-end task from
perception to motion forecasting,

00:03:06.821 --> 00:03:09.000
with everything released publicly.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.946
The data was captured with a Clearpath Husky
unmanned ground vehicle,

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:16.707
remotely operated through downtown Seoul.

00:03:16.787 --> 00:03:20.342
The robot carried two sixteen-channel Velodyne LiDARs,

00:03:20.422 --> 00:03:24.180
five Basler cameras arranged to cover a full 360 degrees,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.759
two inertial measurement units, and real-time
kinematic positioning.

00:03:28.839 --> 00:03:31.048
To fuse camera and LiDAR reliably,

00:03:31.128 --> 00:03:38.118
a pulse-per-second signal generator triggers all
the sensors so their data is accurately aligned in time.

00:03:38.198 --> 00:03:44.919
The robot's own pose is recovered from RTK outdoors
and from a LiDAR-inertial SLAM algorithm indoors,

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:51.180
which is needed to compensate for the robot's
ego-motion when forming pedestrian trajectories

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.268
and to align the semantic maps.

00:03:53.348 --> 00:03:59.934
Those maps use a twelve-layer hierarchical
structure built from point clouds and segmented by hand.

00:04:00.014 --> 00:04:03.368
Expert annotators labeled 3D cuboids at five hertz,

00:04:03.448 --> 00:04:06.061
which were interpolated up to ten hertz,

00:04:06.141 --> 00:04:10.774
and 2D boxes were generated from the 3D cuboids
that share object IDs.

00:04:10.854 --> 00:04:14.410
For privacy, all faces and license plates are blurred.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.839
In total, SiT contains 60 scenes,

00:04:16.919 --> 00:04:22.260
which amounts to about sixty thousand images and
twelve thousand point cloud frames,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.938
carrying roughly four hundred and seventy thousand
two-dimensional annotations and three

00:04:28.018 --> 00:04:31.681
hundred and twenty thousand three-dimensional annotations.

00:04:31.761 --> 00:04:36.069
Each clip is twenty seconds of sequential data
sampled at ten hertz,

00:04:36.149 --> 00:04:40.263
producing nine seconds of trajectory represented as
pose vectors.

00:04:40.343 --> 00:04:43.812
On top of this data, the paper defines four benchmarks.

00:04:43.892 --> 00:04:51.168
Three-D pedestrian detection is scored with average
precision based on center distance at thresholds of a quarter,

00:04:51.248 --> 00:04:52.975
a half, one, and two meters.

00:04:53.055 --> 00:04:56.911
Three-D tracking uses standard multi-object
tracking metrics.

00:04:56.991 --> 00:05:02.590
Trajectory prediction takes two seconds of past
motion and predicts seven seconds ahead,

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:08.462
scored with average and final displacement error
over the best of K candidate trajectories.

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:14.850
And an end-to-end task runs from raw sensors all
the way to future bounding boxes and trajectories.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:20.637
The data analysis confirms that SiT captures
exactly the interactions it set out to.

00:05:20.717 --> 00:05:26.124
When you plot where pedestrians appear relative to
the robot, they cluster close in,

00:05:26.204 --> 00:05:27.431
from all directions,

00:05:27.511 --> 00:05:33.506
whereas in Waymo Open and nuScenes pedestrians tend
to sit off to the side on separate paths.

00:05:33.586 --> 00:05:40.430
Counting instances that satisfy both a
space-sharing condition and a within-two-meter proximity condition,

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:44.938
SiT has dramatically more than the three
autonomous-driving datasets,

00:05:45.018 --> 00:05:52.189
which means driving data underrepresents the
human-robot interaction that social navigation robots must handle.

00:05:52.269 --> 00:05:53.756
On the benchmark itself,

00:05:53.836 --> 00:06:00.485
the standout finding is that supplying the semantic
map improves trajectory prediction: the best model,

00:06:00.565 --> 00:06:01.856
NSP-SFM with the map,

00:06:01.936 --> 00:06:06.298
reaches an ADE-twenty of about 0.52 and an
FDE-twenty of about 0.93,

00:06:06.378 --> 00:06:09.630
clearly better than the same model without the map.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:14.535
The most informative comparison in the paper is
turning the semantic map on and off for trajectory prediction.

00:06:14.615 --> 00:06:19.141
For Y-Net,

00:06:19.221 --> 00:06:23.746
adding the map lowers the ADE-twenty from about
0.84 down to 0.68 and the FDE-twenty from about 1.88 to 1.55.

00:06:23.826 --> 00:06:28.352
For NSP-SFM,

00:06:28.432 --> 00:06:32.957
the map brings ADE-twenty from about 0.63 to 0.52
and FDE-twenty from about 1.09 to 0.93.

00:06:33.037 --> 00:06:37.563
In both cases the map helps,

00:06:37.643 --> 00:06:42.168
confirming the value of the scene context that SiT
provides.

00:06:42.248 --> 00:06:46.774
On the detection side,

00:06:46.854 --> 00:06:51.379
models that fuse camera and LiDAR outperform
single-sensor models,

00:06:51.459 --> 00:06:55.985
and voxel-based backbones outperform pillar-based ones,

00:06:56.065 --> 00:07:00.590
with TransFusion using a voxel backbone giving the
best detection score.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:04.960
Here are the numbers worth remembering.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.030
SiT has sixty scenes,

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.100
about sixty thousand images and twelve thousand
point cloud frames,

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:17.170
with roughly four hundred and seventy thousand
two-dimensional and three hundred and twenty

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:21.240
thousand three-dimensional annotations.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.310
On the trajectory benchmark,

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:29.380
the best model with the semantic map reaches an
ADE-twenty of about 0.52 and an FDE-twenty of about 0.93.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.450
On detection,

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.520
the best camera-plus-LiDAR fusion model reaches a
mean average precision of about 0.53.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.590
And on tracking, the CenterPoint tracker leads with
an sAMOTA around 0.61.

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:43.282
The takeaway is simple.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:47.700
If you want to build robots that move safely and
gracefully among people,

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:51.694
you need training data that shows the robot up
close in the crowd,

00:07:51.774 --> 00:07:56.899
with all its sensors aligned in time and with the
surrounding scene captured as a map.

00:07:56.979 --> 00:08:00.106
SiT is the first dataset to deliver that combination,

00:08:00.186 --> 00:08:04.282
together with a benchmark that spans detection,
tracking, prediction,

00:08:04.362 --> 00:08:05.916
and end-to-end forecasting,

00:08:05.996 --> 00:08:09.850
and it is all publicly released so the community
can build on it.
